            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.euronews.com/video/2026/10/28/spain-first-footage-reveals-wreckage-of-deadly-high-speed-train-crash">https://www.euronews.com/video/2026/10/28/spain-first-footage-reveals-wreckage-of-deadly-high-speed-train-crash</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50272596">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="js-replaceable-video-article-details" data-obfuscatelink="1">
      <div>
                            <p>
              Updated: <time datetime="2026-10-28T14:38:28+01:00">28 October 2026 - 14:38 GMT+1</time>
          </p>
              </div>
      <div>                    <h2>At least 10 people died when a high-speed train struck another near Toledo, Spain. Two carriages derailed, leaving rescuers searching through the wreckage as authorities warned the death toll could rise.</h2>
    <div>
          <p>The crash occurred between <strong>Urda and Los Yébenes, in Toledo province</strong>, when a train travelling from Madrid towards Seville <strong>struck the rear of another that had stopped on the same track</strong>. <strong>Two carriages were knocked off the rails</strong>, one of them torn apart, with twisted metal and debris scattered down a gravel embankment. Aerial footage shows the damaged trains surrounded by firefighters, police and rescue teams in a flat, rural landscape. Castilla-La Mancha government spokeswoman Esther Padilla <strong>confirmed 10 deaths but warned that the toll was likely to rise</strong>.</p><div>
        <div>
        
        <p><img src="https://www.euronews.com/images/logos/logo-euronews-stacked-outlined-72x72-grey-9.svg" width="72" height="72" alt="">
          <span>ADVERTISEMENT</span>
        </p>
    </div>

        <div>
        
        <p><img src="https://www.euronews.com/images/logos/logo-euronews-stacked-outlined-72x72-grey-9.svg" width="72" height="72" alt="">
          <span>ADVERTISEMENT</span>
        </p>
    </div>

</div>
<p>The collision involved a <strong>RENFE train carrying 397 passengers</strong> and an <strong>IRYO service with 147 people</strong> on board. The <strong>cause remains unknown</strong>. Emergency workers concentrated on reaching victims in the two worst-damaged carriages, while injured passengers were taken to nearby hospitals. A <strong>military emergency unit was deployed</strong>, and <strong>rail services between Madrid, Ciudad Real and Andalusia were suspended</strong>.</p>
<p>Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez <strong>cancelled his engagements to focus on the disaster</strong>. It was <strong>Spain's second major rail tragedy of 2026</strong>, following a <strong>January collision in southern Spain that killed 45 people</strong>. That earlier accident had already raised concerns about safety on the country's extensive high-speed rail network.</p>
      </div>

                </div>
    </div></div>]]></description>
