        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Git for US Legislation (379 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://lexifina.com/blog/git-for-the-us-code</link>
            <guid>50268380</guid>
            <pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 01:47:50 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://lexifina.com/blog/git-for-the-us-code">https://lexifina.com/blog/git-for-the-us-code</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50268380">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><figure></figure><p>A programmer can point at any line of code and see who wrote it, when, and what the file looked like on any past day. A lawyer reading a federal statute usually cannot, at least not without an afternoon in the notes at the foot of the section. But why shouldn't they have the same, convenient system of record? It is especially relevant given that so much first-pass legal research now runs through language models, which are often unable to utilise the proper lineage of a law, handcuffing its ability to offer interpretation.</p><h2 id="us-code">The US code</h2><p>Outside the titles Congress has enacted as positive law, the code is only prima facie evidence of the law, and the Statutes at Large govern where the two differ.<sup><span><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->1<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> The Office of the Law Revision Counsel publishes it as a series of release points, each current through a named public law, and keeps reorganising it; a bill to enact title 41 as positive law was introduced in September 2025.<sup><span><a href="#ref-2" aria-label="Reference 2" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->2<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> Which public law wrote a sentence is recorded in amendment notes, in prose. When a change took effect is often in a separate effective-date note, and where Congress said nothing the default rule is that a law takes effect on the day it is enacted.<sup><span><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->3<!-- -->]</a></span></sup></p><p>The definition of a small business debtor in 11 USC § 1182(1) shows how far apart those answers can be. It was created in 2019 with the existing small business debt limit.<sup><span><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->4<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> The CARES Act raised the limit to $7.5 million in March 2020 as a temporary measure, later extended to March 2022.<sup><span><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->5<!-- -->]</a></span><span>,<a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->6<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> That increase lapsed on 27 March 2022, and on 21 June 2022 Congress restored it for two more years, for cases commenced on or after 27 March 2020 and still pending, which reached back over the gap.<sup><span><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->7<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> The higher limit expired again in June 2024, and after the triennial adjustment in April 2025 it stands at $3,424,000.<sup><span><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->8<!-- -->]</a></span><span>,<a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->9<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> For a case filed in May 2022, the release point printed at the time gives the lower limit, and only a later Act changes the answer. A history that is useful to a lawyer has to carry the text, the date each change took force, and the scope of any law that reaches back.</p><p>A study from the Technical University of Munich this year found two failures in models answering questions on German statutes: they apply rules that were superseded after their training cutoff, and they prefer the newer provision even when an older one governs the facts. One model answered 24% of post-cutoff questions correctly on its own and 87% when the relevant statutory text was retrieved for it, and the authors conclude that temporal validity has to be treated as a hard constraint.<sup><span><a href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->10<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> Other recent work trains search agents to respect the date a question is about, with large gains in consistency.<sup><span><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->11<!-- -->]</a></span></sup></p><p>Asking a model to produce the old text does not solve this. When the same Munich group had a model apply German amending laws to earlier versions, the output looked 93–99% similar to the official text, but only half of single amendments and a fifth of chains of amendments matched exactly, and experts found legally significant errors in the near misses.<sup><span><a href="#ref-12" aria-label="Reference 12" data-hover-effect="none">[<!-- -->12<!-- -->]</a></span></sup> The history has to be checkable.</p><p>For each pair of release points from 2017 onward, we took the public laws enacted in between, carried out their amending instructions on the older text in date order, and compared the result with the newer version. Of 365 changes across the nine titles, 361 reproduced the next release point exactly. The other four are shown with a label saying how far they could be confirmed. Each change then carries the law and the date it took effect: 261 took effect on enactment by default, 72 by an effective-date note and 29 by a date in the amendment itself. One depends on an agency determination that may never come, and we leave it out of the text rather than guess.</p><p>With that record, the reader can answer the questions lawyers ask. You pick a date and get the text in force on it, with the words that read differently today marked. Each phrase is coloured by the Act that wrote it. A change to a defined term lists every other provision that uses it, and you can follow a section to see what has changed since your last visit.</p><h2 id="limits">Limits</h2><p>It does not yet show a later law’s reach back on the earlier dates it covers, so for a case filed in the 2022 gap it shows the lower limit without warning that the June 2022 Act changed the answer.</p><p>Effective-date notes are read by pattern, and unusual wording falls back to the date of enactment.</p><p>Each change links to the law’s own words, and for anything that matters the original should be read.</p><p>We're also still thinking about the right form factor this should take.</p><h2 id="references">References</h2><ol><li id="ref-1">1 USC § 204(a)<!-- --> <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/1/204" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/1/204<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-2">Office of the Law Revision Counsel, ‘Positive Law Codification: Title 41’ (H.R. 5185, 119th Congress, introduced 8 September 2025)<!-- --> <a href="https://uscode.house.gov/codification/t41/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->uscode.house.gov/codification/t41/index.html<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-3"><i>Gozlon-Peretz v United States</i> 498 US 395, 404 (1991)<!-- --> <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/498/395" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/498/395<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-4">Small Business Reorganization Act of 2019, Pub L 116-54<!-- --> <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-116publ54/html/PLAW-116publ54.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-116publ54/html/PLAW-116publ54.htm<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-5">Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Security Act, Pub L 116-136, s 1113<!-- --> <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-116publ136/html/PLAW-116publ136.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-116publ136/html/PLAW-116publ136.htm<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-6">COVID-19 Bankruptcy Relief Extension Act of 2021, Pub L 117-5<!-- --> <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ5/html/PLAW-117publ5.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ5/html/PLAW-117publ5.htm<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-7">Bankruptcy Threshold Adjustment and Technical Corrections Act, Pub L 117-151, s 2(h)–(i)<!-- --> <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ151/html/PLAW-117publ151.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ151/html/PLAW-117publ151.htm<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-8">Judicial Conference of the United States, ‘Adjustment of Certain Dollar Amounts Applicable to Bankruptcy Cases’ 90 Fed Reg 8941 (4 February 2025)<!-- --> <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-02-04/html/2025-02207.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-02-04/html/2025-02207.htm<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-9">US Trustee Program, ‘Subchapter V’ (US Department of Justice)<!-- --> <a href="https://www.justice.gov/ust/subchapter-v" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->www.justice.gov/ust/subchapter-v<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-10">Max Prior, Andreas Schultz and Matthias Grabmair, ‘Asking For An Old Friend: Diagnosing and Mitigating Temporal Failure Modes in LLM-based Statutory Question Answering’ (arXiv:2605.23497, 22 May 2026)<!-- --> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23497" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->arxiv.org/abs/2605.23497<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-11">Wei Fan and others, ‘Can LLMs Time Travel? Enhancing Temporal Consistency in Legal Agentic Search through Reinforcement Learning’ (arXiv:2605.25920, revised 19 September 2026)<!-- --> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25920" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->arxiv.org/abs/2605.25920<!-- -->&gt;</a></li><li id="ref-12">Max Prior, Adrian Hof, Niklas Wais and Matthias Grabmair, ‘Risks and Limits of Automatic Consolidation of Statutes’ in <i>Proceedings of the Natural Legal Language Processing Workshop 2025</i> (ACL, November 2025) 396<!-- --> <a href="https://aclanthology.org/2025.nllp-1.29/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-hover-effect="none">&lt;<!-- -->aclanthology.org/2025.nllp-1.29/<!-- -->&gt;</a></li></ol><figure data-blog-visual="true"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A pair of scales drawn in section and paragraph signs, swinging gently, with a red weight in one pan"></canvas></figure></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Reviewers rarely talk about reliability. Owners bring it up twice as often (110 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.bettascore.ai/research/reliability-reviewers-vs-owners</link>
            <guid>50266780</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:31:15 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.bettascore.ai/research/reliability-reviewers-vs-owners">https://www.bettascore.ai/research/reliability-reviewers-vs-owners</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50266780">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><article id="top"><figure><figcaption>Reliability, durability and support: share of each side's opinions</figcaption><div role="list" aria-label="Share of opinions about reliability, by category"><p><span>Self-cleaning litter boxes</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">3%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">39%</b></span></p><p><span>Laser engravers</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">14%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">41%</b></span></p><p><span>Robot lawn mowers</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">9%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">27%</b></span></p><p><span>Robot vacuums</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">1%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">18%</b></span></p><p><span>Robot pool cleaners</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">9%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">25%</b></span></p><p><span>3D printers</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">11%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">22%</b></span></p><p><span>Portable power stations</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">10%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">21%</b></span></p><p><span>Electric bikes</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">5%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">11%</b></span></p><p><span>Electric scooters</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">13%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">19%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart glasses</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">1%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">5%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart locks</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">6%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">10%</b></span></p><p><span>Coffee machines</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">17%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">18%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart rings</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">10%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">11%</b></span></p><p><span>Tennis ball machines</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">16%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">13%</b></span></p></div><details><summary>The numbers behind the chart</summary><div><table><caption>Share of opinions about reliability, durability and support, by category and platform</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Reviewers (YouTube)</th><th scope="col">Owners (Reddit)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Self-cleaning litter boxes</th><td>3%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->5<!-- --> of <!-- -->181<!-- -->)</small></td><td>39%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->23<!-- --> of <!-- -->59<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Laser engravers</th><td>14%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->15<!-- --> of <!-- -->106<!-- -->)</small></td><td>41%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->30<!-- --> of <!-- -->74<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Robot lawn mowers</th><td>9%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->23<!-- --> of <!-- -->259<!-- -->)</small></td><td>27%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->32<!-- --> of <!-- -->117<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Robot vacuums</th><td>1%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->12<!-- --> of <!-- -->975<!-- -->)</small></td><td>18%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->84<!-- --> of <!-- -->466<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Robot pool cleaners</th><td>9%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->31<!-- --> of <!-- -->337<!-- -->)</small></td><td>25%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->13<!-- --> of <!-- -->53<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3D printers</th><td>11%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->87<!-- --> of <!-- -->762<!-- -->)</small></td><td>22%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->103<!-- --> of <!-- -->465<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Portable power stations</th><td>10%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->113<!-- --> of <!-- -->1,126<!-- -->)</small></td><td>21%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->57<!-- --> of <!-- -->275<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Electric bikes</th><td>5%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->75<!-- --> of <!-- -->1,527<!-- -->)</small></td><td>11%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->84<!-- --> of <!-- -->782<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Electric scooters</th><td>13%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->71<!-- --> of <!-- -->551<!-- -->)</small></td><td>19%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->61<!-- --> of <!-- -->329<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Smart glasses</th><td>1%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->2<!-- --> of <!-- -->276<!-- -->)</small></td><td>5%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->11<!-- --> of <!-- -->215<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Smart locks</th><td>6%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->10<!-- --> of <!-- -->170<!-- -->)</small></td><td>10%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->11<!-- --> of <!-- -->109<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Coffee machines</th><td>17%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->63<!-- --> of <!-- -->375<!-- -->)</small></td><td>18%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->70<!-- --> of <!-- -->394<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Smart rings</th><td>10%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->21<!-- --> of <!-- -->205<!-- -->)</small></td><td>11%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->15<!-- --> of <!-- -->138<!-- -->)</small></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tennis ball machines</th><td>16%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->8<!-- --> of <!-- -->50<!-- -->)</small></td><td>13%<!-- --> <small>(<!-- -->12<!-- --> of <!-- -->91<!-- -->)</small></td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></figure><p>Across these <!-- -->14<!-- --> categories: <!-- -->7.8%<!-- --> of reviewer opinions vs<!-- --> <!-- -->17.0%<!-- --> of owner opinions.<!-- --> <!-- -->Owners bring it up more in 13 of the 14; tennis ball machines are the exception, with only 91 owner opinions.<!-- --> <!-- -->6,900<!-- --> opinions from YouTube reviews and <!-- -->3,567<!-- --> from Reddit threads.<!-- --> Left out: video doorbells, smart thermostats, mechanical keyboards and 3D scanners, where reliability isn't a topic of its own (it's folded into a broader one such as "Connection, Reliability &amp; Support")<!-- -->, and pickleball machines, which have no Reddit threads yet.</p><div><section id="after-the-review"><h2>Most of what breaks shows up after the review is done</h2><div><ul><li><strong>Robot vacuums:</strong> reliability comes up in <!-- -->1%<!-- --> of reviewer opinions and<!-- --> <!-- -->18%<!-- --> of owner opinions. A review covers a few weeks; the failures owners write about tend to come months later.</li><li><strong>Self-cleaning litter boxes:</strong> <!-- -->3%<!-- --> vs <!-- -->39%<!-- -->. Owners mostly describe breakdowns and how long a replacement takes.</li><li>When owners do bring it up, <!-- -->70%<!-- --> of what they say is negative. Reviewers who mention it are negative <!-- -->15%<!-- --> of the time.</li></ul></div></section><section id="apps"><h2>Apps: <!-- -->the same attention, the opposite verdict</h2><div><p>Reviewers and owners talk about apps, software and connectivity<!-- --> <!-- -->about equally often<!-- --> (<!-- -->8.2%<!-- --> vs <!-- -->9.4%<!-- --> of opinions). They don't agree on them:<!-- --> <!-- -->21%<!-- --> of reviewer opinions on apps are negative, compared with <!-- -->71%<!-- --> of owner opinions.</p><figure><figcaption>Apps, software and connectivity: share of opinions that are negative</figcaption><div role="list" aria-label="Share of negative opinions about apps, by category"><p><span>Mechanical keyboards</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">17%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">75%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart locks</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">17%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">72%</b></span></p><p><span>Portable power stations</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">15%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">69%</b></span></p><p><span>3D scanners</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">28%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">72%</b></span></p><p><span>Robot vacuums</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">9%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">50%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart rings</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">42%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">75%</b></span></p><p><span>Smart glasses</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">38%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">71%</b></span></p><p><span>3D printers</span><span><span>Reviewers </span><b data-platform="youtube">61%</b> · <span>owners </span><b data-platform="reddit">85%</b></span></p></div><p>Categories with at least <!-- -->20<!-- --> positive or negative owner opinions on apps.<!-- --> <!-- -->Owners are more negative in all eight.</p></figure><p>Two products show where this ends up:</p><ul><li><strong>A <!-- -->$7,999<!-- --> laser engraver <!-- -->ranks last of 9<!-- -->.</strong> The<!-- --> <a href="https://www.bettascore.ai/product/glowforge-pro">Glowforge Pro</a> gets 8 positive and 0 negative opinions on setup and ease of use.<!-- --> <!-- -->It loses on software and connectivity (<!-- -->3 positive, 7 negative<!-- -->)<!-- --> and on reliability and support (2 positive, 7 negative)<!-- -->. Owners mostly object to a paid software subscription and needing an internet connection to use the machine.</li><li><strong>A <!-- -->$350<!-- --> smart lock <!-- -->ranks last of 20<!-- -->.</strong> The<!-- --> <a href="https://www.bettascore.ai/product/eufy-familock-s3">Eufy FamiLock S3</a> has <!-- -->0<!-- --> <!-- -->positive and <!-- -->7<!-- --> negative opinions on its app and smart-home features, mostly about a firmware update that left locks unusable.<!-- --> All 10 of its sources are Reddit threads.</li></ul></div></section><section id="before-you-buy"><h2>If you're about to buy</h2><div><ul><li>Search owner threads for the model plus "months" or "a year later", not just the launch reviews.</li><li>Check whether the device still works if the app or the company's servers go away.</li><li>Look at the warranty length and how owners describe getting a replacement.</li></ul></div></section><section id="before-the-numbers"><h2>Read this before the numbers</h2><div><ul><li><strong>Reviewers aren't hiding anything.</strong> Most reviews are made in the first days or weeks with a new unit. That's too early for most failures, and it's hard to test a company's support.</li><li><strong>Owners post when something goes wrong.</strong> Nobody starts a thread to say their robot vacuum still works, so these numbers show what owners worry about, not how often things break. For scale: across all topics,<!-- --> <!-- -->56%<!-- --> of owner opinions and <!-- -->15%<!-- --> of reviewer opinions are negative. Reliability (<!-- -->70%<!-- -->) and apps (<!-- -->71%<!-- -->) are still well above the owners' own average.</li><li><strong>Topic grouping matters.</strong> If we also count mixed topics such as "Value &amp; Support" or "Responsiveness &amp; Key Reliability", owners bring up reliability <!-- -->2.4<!-- --> times as often<!-- --> instead of twice<!-- -->, and more in<!-- --> <!-- -->all 17 categories that have owner opinions<!-- -->. We lead with the stricter number. The same goes for apps: mixed topics like "Connectivity &amp; Battery" are left out.</li><li><strong>Some YouTube reviews are sponsored.</strong> We count them like any other source and can't reliably tell which ones are.</li><li><strong>An AI model labels each opinion</strong> with a topic and as positive or negative. Every opinion links back to the sentence it came from, so any of them can be checked.</li></ul></div></section><section id="method"><h2>Method</h2><div><ul><li><strong>Sources:</strong> YouTube reviews and Reddit threads about <!-- -->657<!-- --> products currently on sale in<!-- --> <!-- -->19<!-- --> categories, collected for BettaScore's rankings: <!-- -->8,062<!-- --> opinions from YouTube and <!-- -->4,525<!-- --> from Reddit. Review sites, forums and Hacker News (<!-- -->517<!-- --> <!-- -->opinions) aren't in the comparison.</li><li><strong>What counts as an opinion:</strong> one source's view on one topic of one product. A source that covers five topics counts five times; a source that mentions a topic ten times counts once.</li><li><strong>Topic groups:</strong> reliability is any topic about reliability, durability, warranty, service or support; apps is any topic about apps, software, firmware or connectivity. Mixed topics are left out of both.</li><li><strong>Shares:</strong> opinions in a topic group ÷ all opinions from that platform, including neutral ones. Negative shares count positive and negative opinions only.</li></ul></div></section><section id="about"><h2>About BettaScore</h2><p>BettaScore ranks products by what public reviews say about them. No brand pays to be listed or to rank higher, and our shopping links earn nothing.</p></section></div><div aria-labelledby="research-rankings-title" data-phase="idle" id="rankings"><p>The rankings</p><h2 id="research-rankings-title">Now see who comes out on top</h2><p>Each ranking shows what owners and reviewers say about every model, reliability included, with the quotes behind each score.</p></div></article></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Hunger beats tenure: Advice for junior engineers (104 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://felixrieseberg.com/hunger-beats-tenure-advice-for-junior-engineers/</link>
            <guid>50266719</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:25:23 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://felixrieseberg.com/hunger-beats-tenure-advice-for-junior-engineers/">https://felixrieseberg.com/hunger-beats-tenure-advice-for-junior-engineers/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50266719">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-slug="hunger-beats-tenure-advice-for-junior-engineers" data-date="2026-10-27"><figure><img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/89/e0/89e08557-681a-40be-8264-278bf46d6b25/content/images/size/w2000/2026/10/IMG_8925.jpeg" srcset="https://storage.ghost.io/c/89/e0/89e08557-681a-40be-8264-278bf46d6b25/content/images/size/w1000/2026/10/IMG_8925.jpeg 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/89/e0/89e08557-681a-40be-8264-278bf46d6b25/content/images/size/w1400/2026/10/IMG_8925.jpeg 1400w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/89/e0/89e08557-681a-40be-8264-278bf46d6b25/content/images/size/w2000/2026/10/IMG_8925.jpeg 2000w" sizes="(min-width: 1080px) 760px, calc(100vw - 32px)" alt="Hunger beats tenure: Advice for junior engineers"></figure><p>This month, I laid the traditional coding interview to rest. As a hiring manager at Anthropic, I stopped asking candidates to do them. It had a good run: Being able to quickly define problems and find elegant solutions was a weak but useful predictor for how successful you’d be on my teams. Small coding challenges in real code did tell us something meaningful about candidates.</p><p>That’s no longer true. There’s no more point in giving you a little React challenge than there is in quizzing people about assembly; Claude is now better at both. Our most impactful builders are successful regardless of how good they are at these tests.</p><p>The coding interview was, despite its ubiquity, never popular. I suspect that established engineers will still have strong reactions to its disappearance — if only for what it says about the evolution of our roles. Plenty of ink has been spilled to describe how AI is a challenge for junior developers, especially those trying to land a job. Now that I’ve seen junior people work with AI for a bit, I have a more hopeful take: Impact is less bounded by how many years of accumulated knowledge in frameworks and libraries you’ve gathered.</p><p>I’m seeing engineers who are comparatively new fly past people who are probably better coders and have been around much longer. Hunger beats tenure.</p><p>This post is my best advice for junior engineers, though “junior” is the wrong word. It’s really for engineers without social capital, status, or power.</p><h2 id="all-vision-no-voice">All vision, no voice</h2><p>First, know both your advantage and handicap.</p><p>Fresh eyes, even naive ones, have long been considered powerful. When I joined Stripe, founder Patrick Collison implored us newcomers to pay close attention to things that seem odd to us. His bet was that newcomers would spot friction and opportunities that old-timers had long stopped noticing.</p><p>In 1991, James March <a href="https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2.1.71?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">wrote</a> that organizations depend on “maintaining an influx of the naive and ignorant”. When Orson Welles was asked how he got the confidence to try shots nobody had tried, he <a href="https://www.themarginalian.org/2012/04/30/orson-welles-on-ignorance-1960/?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">said</a>: “Ignorance, sheer ignorance — you know there’s no confidence to equal it. It’s only when you know something about a profession, I think, that you’re timid or careful.”</p><p>When I joined Notion, one of my interviews involved designing an offline-mode architecture. I was baffled to hear that they considered it a “trick” interview, since an offline mode was deemed impossible. The data model was too complex for easily syncing an entire Notion workspace, and downloading individual pages was a user experience CEO Ivan Zhao would never allow. The data model complexity turned out to be true; the founder resistance didn’t. People considered it such common knowledge that nobody had checked. I, an ignorant newcomer, didn’t know better than to just ask.</p><p>It’s easy to read anecdotes like this one and see people making bad decisions. I cannot overstate how hard it is to spot these mistakes when you’re the one making them. Shortly after joining Notion, I wanted to outfit teams with large TVs to display live dashboards. I ran into organizational walls, failed, and didn’t care enough to start a whole fight.</p><p>A year later, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ji-pei?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Ji Pei</a> joined as a new hire with the same idea. I scoffed and told him not to bother; I’d already tried. He’d already asked. The TVs showed up the next day. Somewhere in that year, I had become the old-timer.</p><p>The only problem with all that ignorant power: Nobody cares what you think. Most tech companies have layers of middle management to translate larger business goals into execution. Structurally, companies are often built around the idea of a few people green-lighting or blocking individual ideas. In a world where execution is expensive, you normally have to grind your way to some social status before the entire organization would “take a bet” on your idea. Org charts tend to change at a glacial pace, so even companies that believe in AI often expect junior people to simply execute their tasks faster rather than ambitiously go after business goals.</p><p>Well, good news: Execution isn’t expensive anymore.</p><h2 id="what-to-do">What to do</h2><p>(1) Pick business impact over backlog: In pre-AI-coding 2011, Patrick McKenzie <a href="https://www.kalzumeus.com/2011/10/28/dont-call-yourself-a-programmer/?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">wrote</a> <a href="https://www.kalzumeus.com/2011/10/28/dont-call-yourself-a-programmer/?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">“engineers are hired to create business value, not to program things.”</a> Attach yourself, he advised, to the parts of a company that make money. Start there: Understand what your company is actually trying to accomplish (or, at a larger one, what your group or department is). Then find the right altitude — understanding what kind of goals your manager’s manager has is often a useful proxy.</p><p>(2) Pick a radical idea: Come up with a few ideas that would, if successful, dramatically improve the chances that your organization achieves those goals. Be ambitious!</p><p>(3) Build it over a weekend: I have seen small groups and solo engineers fly past the usual process of writing product requirements documents (PRDs), getting buy-in, and lobbying for time with designers or product managers by building a working prototype.</p><p>(4) Focus on one good idea: In my experience, junior people often make the mistake of cramming multiple ideas into a single prototype, hiding three excellent ideas among 20 bad ones. If your prototype involves the creation of a new programming language, a backend, and also somehow mobile apps, you’re not going to be successful. The point of creating a working prototype is to communicate an idea so well that leadership quickly understands both the opportunity and that you’re the one who can run with it.</p><p>(5) Lean into your fresh perspective: If you’re working on something user-facing, you have a unique amount of empathy for users and thus a much better sense for how the product works or doesn’t work for them. My colleagues at Slack and Notion had a hard time internalizing what using Slack or Notion is like as an external user.</p><p>If you’re working on infrastructure or tooling, you have the benefit of never having learned all the reasons things are considered “impossible”.</p><h2 id="the-future-is-bright">The future is bright</h2><p>I don’t think the coding interview is going to come back. It’s easy to get sentimental given how many copies of “Algorithms and Data Structures” were bought in hopes of unlocking good jobs and a better future — or how the death of the coding interview is another milestone in software engineering reaching a new level of abstraction.</p><p>At the same time, as a longtime hater of promotions by tenure, I can’t help but be optimistic. I’ve already seen my fair share of popular apps and products made this way.</p><p>At Anthropic, people got so used to using Claude Code as a command-line interface (CLI) that it took two relatively new engineers (<a href="https://x.com/amorriscode?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Anthony Morris</a> and <a href="https://x.com/alicelovescake1?ref=felixrieseberg.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Alice Zhao</a>) to whip up a working prototype over one weekend. The first public version shipped roughly eight days later. You won’t be surprised to hear that a good graphical interface for Claude Code is a very popular product.</p><p>I'm probably not the only manager trying to figure out how to interview for people who build well with AI. I haven't quite gotten there yet — but I'm scanning resumes and portfolios for little prototypes that exhibit taste, empathy, and principled thinking. If you have figured out something better, I'd love to hear how.</p><p>The image for this post is by J. M. W. Turner, “<a href="https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/joseph-mallord-william-turner-the-fighting-temeraire?ref=felixrieseberg.com">The Fighting Temeraire</a>” (1839)</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
