        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Survival LLM: AI assistant and emergency library that runs on your own computer (479 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.brangus.io/survival-llm</link>
            <guid>50264853</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 19:55:06 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.brangus.io/survival-llm">https://www.brangus.io/survival-llm</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50264853">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
  <section>
    
    <p>Survival LLM is a free AI assistant and emergency library that runs entirely on your own computer.
      Ask it how to keep food safe in a blackout, treat a burn, or purify water, and it answers from
      trusted sources and shows you exactly where each answer came from.</p>
    
    <p id="device-note">Free and open source. Set it up while you have internet; after that it needs none.</p>
    <img src="https://www.brangus.io/survival-llm/screenshot.png" alt="Survival LLM answering a question about fridge food safety, with the cited Ready.gov article open beside it">
  </section>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <div>
    <div><h3>Works with no internet</h3><p>The AI model and the library live on your computer. Nothing you ask leaves it.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Shows its sources</h3><p>Every answer lists the articles it read. Click one to read it yourself, with the exact passage highlighted.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Made for emergencies</h3><p>Medical references, FEMA and CDC guidance, survival manuals, how-to guides, repair guides and about 930,000 Wikipedia articles.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="download">Pick your version</h2>
  <p>The app is the same everywhere. When it opens, it checks your computer's memory and downloads the AI model that fits.
    More memory means a smarter model.</p>
  <div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Your computer</th><th>AI model it uses</th><th>Our emergency test</th><th>Model download</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody id="tiers">
      <tr><td>Mac with 128 GB or more memory</td><td>NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Super</td><td>21 of 22 right</td><td>80 GB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mac with 32–96 GB</td><td>Qwen3.8 27B</td><td>21 of 22 right</td><td>18 GB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Windows PC with 32 GB or more</td><td>NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Lightning</td><td>19 of 22 right</td><td>25 GB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mac or PC with 16–31 GB</td><td>IBM Granite 4.2 8B</td><td>14 of 22 right</td><td>6 GB</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>"Right" means it got the main point of official guidance right on 22 blackout and first-aid
    questions, graded blind, in our tests on an earlier build of the library (<a id="tests-link" href="#source">how we
    tested</a>). Smaller models make more mistakes, so the app always shows the source to check.</p>
  <p>Plus the library: a <span id="lib-full">9 GB</span> download for everything (19 GB once installed), or
    <span id="lib-lite">1.3 GB</span> (3 GB installed) without Wikipedia and iFixit. Free disk space needed: the model's
    size plus 5 GB, plus about 27 GB while the full library installs (10 GB for the smaller one).
    Apple Silicon Macs (M1 or newer) with macOS 14 Sonoma or newer, and 64-bit Windows 10 or 11.</p>

  <h2>Set it up in three steps</h2>
  <p>Setup takes 20 minutes to about 2½ hours, depending on your computer and your internet speed. You only click for
    about 5 minutes; the rest is downloading.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Download Survival LLM</strong> for <a id="dl-mac-2" href="#download">Mac</a> or <a id="dl-win-2" href="#download">Windows</a>
      and unzip it. On Windows, right-click the zip and choose <em>Extract All</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Open it.</strong> On a Mac, double-click <em>Survival LLM</em>. On Windows, open the extracted folder and
      double-click <em>Survival LLM.bat</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow the setup.</strong> The app opens in its own window and downloads the AI engine (built into the Mac
      app), the AI model that fits your computer, and the library. Let it finish while you're online.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>The first time you open it,</strong> your computer warns you, because the app isn't sold through an app store.
    <br><strong>Mac:</strong> click <em>Done</em>, open <em>System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security</em>, scroll down to
    "Survival LLM was blocked", click <em>Open Anyway</em> and confirm with your password. You only do this once.
    <br><strong>Windows:</strong> click <em>More info</em>, then <em>Run anyway</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>What's in the library</h2>
  <p>Plain-text copies of openly licensed (some for non-commercial use only) and public-domain references, searchable
    in a fraction of a second:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>About 930,000 of Wikipedia's most-read articles</li>
    <li>WikiMed medical encyclopedia</li>
    <li>MedlinePlus (U.S. National Library of Medicine)</li>
    <li>UK Medicines A to Z (public sector information)</li>
    <li>WikEM emergency medicine</li>
    <li>Ready.gov (FEMA) emergency guides</li>
    <li>CDC travelers' health</li>
    <li>U.S. military medicine and survival manuals</li>
    <li>Risk references for estimating micromorts: life tables and U.S. death rates</li>
    <li>wikiHow how-to guides</li>
    <li>iFixit repair guides</li>
    <li>Appropedia (appropriate technology)</li>
    <li>Q&amp;A on home repair, cooking, gardening, the outdoors and ham radio (Stack Exchange)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each source keeps its own license, shown with every article; see the
    <a id="sources-link" href="#source">full list with licenses</a>.</p>

  <h2>Good to know</h2>
  <div>
    <div><h3>It can be wrong</h3><p>AI models make mistakes, the small ones more often. Every answer shows the articles it used, so you can check.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Private</h3><p>No account, no tracking. Your questions never leave your computer, and once set up it works with the internet off.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Your own files</h3><p>Add PDFs, Word documents, ebooks and notes, like your generator's manual or your family's
      emergency plan, and the AI searches them too. They stay on your computer.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Updates</h3><p>Check for updates, at the bottom of the app, installs a new version in one click and keeps your
      models, library and files.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Battery</h3><p>On a MacBook Pro we measured about 100 W while it answers, for 5 to 40 seconds per question: a full battery
      answers roughly 100 to 300. A small generator or power station runs it easily.</p></div>
  </div>

  
</div></div>]]></description>
